    {
      "dom_id": "question-116727",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14193183/assets/69245c30b32f12e59a5f57f2e8fb17c7374726ffd6db10726cb508727f66c409.png)\n\nFind the measure of angle $B$ given that $m∠A=m∠C.$",
      "worked_solution": "Using $m∠A=m∠C,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A & =m∠C \\\\ 3z+{20}^{∘} & =4z \\\\ z & ={20}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, substituting $z={20}^{∘}$ in the expression for $m∠C,$ we get\n\n$$\nm∠C=4({20}^{∘})={80}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find $m∠B$ using the fact that the internal angles of any triangle sum to ${180}^{∘}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B+2m∠C & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B+2\\cdot {80}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B+{160}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠B & ={20}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [],
      "problem_field_keys": [],
      "answer_fields_complete": null,
